# 25. paste Command

The `paste` command combines lines from multiple files.

Suppose we have:

    names.txt

containing:

    Niranjan
    Rahul
    Arun

And:

    ages.txt

containing:

    21
    22
    20

Run:

    paste names.txt ages.txt

Output:

    Niranjan    21
    Rahul       22
    Arun        20

The first line of the first file is combined with the first line of the second file.

---

# 26. paste with Delimiter

We can specify a delimiter using:

    -d

Example:

    paste -d "," names.txt ages.txt

Output:

    Niranjan,21
    Rahul,22
    Arun,20

This can be useful when creating simple CSV-style data.

---

# 27. head Command

The `head` command displays the beginning of a file.

Example:

    head file.txt

By default, it displays the first 10 lines.

To display a specific number of lines:

    head -n 5 file.txt

This displays the first 5 lines.

---

# 28. Real-World Use of head

Suppose we have a very large log file:

    server.log

The file might contain thousands or millions of lines.

Instead of displaying the entire file:

    cat server.log

we can quickly examine the beginning:

    head server.log

Or:

    head -n 20 server.log

This displays the first 20 lines.

---

# 29. tail Command

The `tail` command displays the end of a file.

Example:

    tail file.txt

By default, it displays the last 10 lines.

To display the last 5 lines:

    tail -n 5 file.txt

---

# 30. tail -f

One of the most useful options of `tail` is:

    tail -f

The `-f` option means follow.

It continuously displays new lines added to a file.

Example:

    tail -f server.log

If a new log entry is added to `server.log`, it will appear on the terminal.

Stop the command using:

    Ctrl + C

---

# 31. Real-World Cybersecurity Example

Web servers generate log files.

For example:

    access.log

We can monitor the latest activity using:

    tail -f access.log

We might see:

    192.168.1.10 GET /login
    192.168.1.15 GET /index.html
    192.168.1.20 POST /login

If new requests arrive, they can appear immediately.

This is useful for:

- Log monitoring
- System administration
- Security monitoring
- Troubleshooting
- Digital forensics

---

# 32. Combining Commands

The real power of Linux comes from combining small commands.

For example:

    cat access.log | grep "ERROR"

This means:

    cat
     |
     v
    grep
     |
     v
    ERROR messages

Another example:

    cat access.log | grep "ERROR" | head

This means:

1. Read the file.
2. Find lines containing `ERROR`.
3. Display the first few matching lines.

